course: dictionary
lesson: 8475
title: Names with a capital letter
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sam is a name, so it starts with a capital letter. A name for one particular person, place or thing is called a proper noun. In a maths word problem, «Sam has 12 marbles and gives 5 away», the name tells you who has the marbles, but the sum is still 12 take away 5, which is 7.
explain: Sam is a short name, often for Samuel, Samantha or Samson. Friends can change it: «Sammy» is casual and friendly. In formal writing, people use the full name, such as «Samuel», or a description like «the first boy».
explain: The words around a name give you clues about who is meant. «Going to Sam's» means going to Sam's house, because the apostrophe shows that the house belongs to Sam. «Uncle Sam» is different: it is a nickname for the government of the USA, not a relative.
fact: A proper noun is the name of a particular person, place or thing, and it starts with a capital letter.
fact: «Sam» is a common first name for boys and girls, short for Samuel, Samantha or Samson.
fact: «Uncle Sam» is a nickname for the government of the USA.
fact: «Sammy» is a casual, friendly way to shorten the name Sam.
fact: In formal writing, people use the full name, such as «Samuel», or a description like «the first boy».
exercise: In «Sam has 12 marbles and gives 5 away», what kind of word is Sam? | A place | *A proper noun, a name | A number | A verb, a doing word
exercise: Why does «Sam» start with a capital letter? | *Because it is a name | Because it is the first word of every sentence | Because it comes at the end of the sentence | Because it is a long word
exercise: In «When Lily's friends leave her alone, Sam sits with her at lunch», what is Sam like? | *A kind friend who keeps her company | A rival who wins every game | A teacher who gives homework | A bus driver who is always late
test: In «Uncle Sam», what is Sam? | A type of sandwich | *A nickname for the government of the USA | A boy who lives next door | A pupil in the class
test: Which is the casual, friendly short form of Sam? | *Sammy | Samuel | Samson | The first boy
exam: Which sentence uses the full name in formal writing? | «Sammy came to the party.» | «Sam-Sam wants to play now.» | *«Samuel attended the meeting and left early.» | «Sam's mum made tea for everyone.»
